<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Burana Tower</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Burana Tower is a brick minaret in the Chuy Valley near Tokmok in northern Kyrgyzstan, built in the eleventh century under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanids</a>. It stands on the site of a medieval town usually identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a>, one of the dynasty's capitals. Originally around 40 metres high, it was cut to about 25 metres by earthquakes and has been partly restored. Stone grave figures (balbals) are gathered at the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/burana-tower.jpg" alt="Burana Tower" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burana_Tower,_Kyrgyzstan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
